<header class="start-masthead"><p class="eyebrow">Lab runtime · proposed documentation</p><h1>Use Paperclip as a bounded Lab reader</h1><p>Configure the pinned process adapter without confusing orchestration with Lab authority.</p><p class="source-note">Draft prepared on 2026-10-14. Integration and final T21 qualification remain pending.</p></header>

<section id="runtime-paperclip-what-has-actually-been-observed" aria-labelledby="runtime-paperclip-what-has-actually-been-observed-title"><h2 id="runtime-paperclip-what-has-actually-been-observed-title">What has actually been observed</h2>

<p>The pinned published Paperclip version is 2026.1005.0. A local process-reader episode was observed on 13 October 2026 through the independently selected wrapper/runner configuration. Its <a href="research/sources/docs/plans/0010-lab-one-shot-execution/runs/0001-candidate-construction/paperclip-local-pilot-0270/closure.json">public closure</a> records process success, zero Lab mutations and zero Lab Sessions. It read a historical genuine-W09 engineering source.</p>

<p>The earlier <a href="research/sources/product/lab/release-candidates/v1/interface/paperclip-runner-v3/README.md">runner README</a> says Paperclip was not installed when that package was authored. The later receipt records setup; neither statement establishes current server health. The recorded local endpoint was 127.0.0.1:3100, the old reader grant expired, and the human pilot/usefulness outcome remains unobserved.</p>

</section>

<section id="runtime-paperclip-keep-the-two-authority-boundaries" aria-labelledby="runtime-paperclip-keep-the-two-authority-boundaries-title"><h2 id="runtime-paperclip-keep-the-two-authority-boundaries-title">Keep the two authority boundaries</h2>

<p>Paperclip company/agent/task IDs and its API token provide orchestration and correlation. Lab access comes from an independently provisioned current Lab policy, reader key, source/profile/plan pins and signed nonce proof. Do not put real private credentials or policy bodies into Paperclip task text, repository files or stdout.</p>

<p>The recorded agent could not create agents/skills or assign tasks, and used manual/on-demand scheduling. A process-adapter success is not a Work completion, an engineering tool effect, a research conclusion or repository admission. A future autonomous Work integration needs its actual workflow/tool/output/authority evidence.</p>

</section>

<section id="runtime-paperclip-configure-only-the-inspected-adapter-contract" aria-labelledby="runtime-paperclip-configure-only-the-inspected-adapter-contract-title"><h2 id="runtime-paperclip-configure-only-the-inspected-adapter-contract-title">Configure only the inspected adapter contract</h2>

<p>The <a href="research/sources/docs/plans/0010-lab-one-shot-execution/runs/0001-candidate-construction/Paperclip-process-adapter-probe-0140.json">published adapter probe</a> selects an executable command with separate args, cwd and timeout, using direct process spawning. Do not paste a newer upstream shell-command example into this pinned version. A version upgrade needs its own compatibility review.</p>

<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Selected runner argument</th><th scope="col">Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>--api-root</td><td>Exact qualified API directory, not the currently changed/ineligible API17</td></tr><tr><td>--source-sha256 / --profile-sha256</td><td>Independently verified selected source and protocol profile</td></tr><tr><td>--policy / --policy-sha256</td><td>Fresh isolated reader policy and exact digest</td></tr><tr><td>--reader-key / --reader-key-sha256</td><td>Matching protected credential selected outside task text</td></tr><tr><td>--plan / --plan-sha256</td><td>Exact bounded read plan</td></tr><tr><td>--timeout-seconds</td><td>Whole-episode bound, at most 900 seconds</td></tr></tbody></table></div>

<p>These are real runner flags, not a fabricated working configuration. The historic plan was limited to Lab status/catalog reads; the runner code supports additional bounded artifact reads only when its selected plan/policy permits them.</p>

</section>

<section id="runtime-paperclip-follow-a-fresh-operator-setup" aria-labelledby="runtime-paperclip-follow-a-fresh-operator-setup-title"><h2 id="runtime-paperclip-follow-a-fresh-operator-setup-title">Follow a fresh operator setup</h2>

<p>First choose a qualified Lab reader and obtain a fresh scope/key/nonce channel. Run its exact approved command directly; preserve public observations and cleanup. Then configure Paperclip&#x27;s process agent to run that same executable/argument selection from the selected repository cwd and compare results. Start a local server only under the accepted installation/service scope.</p>

<p>The current preparation provides no new credential, runnable server config or qualified integrated API. Old public config hashes and receipts cannot regenerate an expired grant. Keep persistent server/company state separate from temporary Lab credential roots; health, setup and Lab authentication need separate current checks.</p>

</section>

<section id="runtime-paperclip-assess-usefulness-with-the-pilot-owner" aria-labelledby="runtime-paperclip-assess-usefulness-with-the-pilot-owner-title"><h2 id="runtime-paperclip-assess-usefulness-with-the-pilot-owner-title">Assess usefulness with the pilot owner</h2>

<p>The owner&#x27;s question is how to use Paperclip to run the Lab; the agreed research question asks which evidence verifies that ability and what remains unproven. After a fresh technical setup, show the actual read output and ask the owner whether it answered those questions. Record time/failures and their own assessment without claiming improvement against an unattempted baseline.</p>

<p>Do not convert read-only process execution into autonomous engineering/research success. <a href="runtime-work-learning.html">Work and evidence</a> explains the missing output/assessment boundaries; <a href="runtime-status.html">status</a> retains the dated pilot limits.</p>

</section>

